# ─── Line-ending policy: LF everywhere ───────────────────────────────────────
# Git auto-detects text vs binary and normalises text files to LF on commit.
# `eol=lf` also forces LF in the working tree on checkout, including on Windows.
# This prevents CRLF drift when contributors edit with Windows-native tools that
# default to CRLF (Notepad, some editor configs, OneDrive sync round-trips).
*               text=auto eol=lf

# ─── Shell scripts: must be LF (CRLF breaks shebang execution) ───────────────
*.sh            text eol=lf

# ─── Source/docs file types: explicit text + LF ──────────────────────────────
*.py            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.css           text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.svg           text eol=lf

# ─── Binary types: never normalise, never line-ending-munge ─────────────────-
*.pdf           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.webp          binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
*.eot           binary
*.zip           binary
*.tar.gz        binary
*.gz            binary
*.pptx          binary
*.docx          binary
*.xlsx          binary

# ─── After landing this file ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Run once on a clean working tree:
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git status
# That will surface any tracked file Git wants to normalise to LF.
